<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- A2 (ADR-0009). Lets HealthKit wake BADAN via HKObserverQuery when the types it reads
	     change, so the on-device repository is reasonably fresh without the user opening the app.
	     This is an entitlement, not a UIBackgroundModes key: HealthKit background delivery is
	     gated *here* and needs no background mode at all (plan §13.6).

	     BUG-BG-01 — read the next paragraph before acting on the one above. This comment used to
	     end with "there is deliberately no `UIBackgroundModes` entry", stated as a property of
	     the app rather than of HealthKit. That was true of HealthKit background delivery and
	     false of BGTaskScheduler, which the same change set added, and the two are gated by
	     different mechanisms:
	       • HealthKit background delivery — entitlement-gated (this file). No background mode.
	       • BGTaskScheduler              — background-mode-gated. `submit(_:)` throws
	         `BGTaskSchedulerError.unavailable` without UIBackgroundModes `fetch` (for
	         BGAppRefreshTask) and `processing` (for BGProcessingTask). The entitlement does
	         nothing for it and BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers only covers `register`.
	     Both modes are now declared in `project.yml`. Conflating the two mechanisms is what let
	     the app ship where no BG task could ever run, so scope any future claim about
	     UIBackgroundModes to the specific capability it describes.

	     What this does NOT change: an observer wake carries no payload and grants no new read
	     access. It only says "something changed"; the anchored re-query that follows reads exactly
	     the types NSHealthShareUsageDescription already names. No data leaves the device as a
	     result, which is why PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy is unchanged by A2.

	     Binding constraint from ADR-0009: the observer completion handler must be called within
	     seconds of every wake regardless of outcome. HealthKit permanently disables background
	     delivery for the app after 3 missed handlers, and there is no way to detect or reverse
	     that from inside the app — so any code that awaits ingestion before completing the
	     handler breaks this entitlement's value silently and for good. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit.background-delivery</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- EPIC-33 (Phase 3D). Shared container between BADAN and the BadanWidgets extension for the
	     bounded AmbientSnapshotDto only (today's ring score, one-sentence narrative, meds-due list,
	     last-night sleep summary — never a database, never raw health values). WidgetKit extensions
	     cannot open the SQLCipher store directly (extension memory limits, key-availability-before-
	     first-unlock, cross-process locking), so this is the ONLY channel between the app and its
	     widgets. Both BADAN.entitlements and BadanWidgets.entitlements must declare the identical
	     group id or the extension silently reads an empty/nonexistent container instead of failing
	     loudly — there is no runtime error for a mismatched group id, only missing data. -->
	<!-- LAB-11 (EPIC-41). Read-only access to HealthKit Clinical Records so the lab notebook can
	     import lab results a provider has already shared into Apple Health.

	     KEY NAME MATTERS, and an earlier attempt got it wrong: the entitlement is
	     `com.apple.developer.healthkit.access`, an ARRAY containing `health-records`. There is no
	     `com.apple.developer.healthkit.access-health-records` key — using that name failed the
	     archive with "not found and could not be included in profile. This likely is not a valid
	     entitlement", which is Xcode correctly saying the KEY is wrong, not that a capability is
	     missing. Ground truth is Xcode's own capability database, which lists this exact
	     key/value pair under the HealthKit capability:
	       /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/SharedFrameworks/DVTPortal.framework/Versions/A/
	       Resources/DVTPortalCachedPortalCapabilities.json

	     That same record says `canRequestFromPortal: false` and `distributionApprovalRequired:
	     false` — so this needs NO separate toggle in Certificates/Identifiers (there is none to
	     find; the App Store Connect API's capability enum contains only HEALTHKIT) and NO
	     pre-approval from Apple. It rides the HealthKit capability already enabled on the App ID.
	     The real gate is ordinary App Review at submission, which is why
	     NSHealthClinicalHealthRecordsShareUsageDescription in project.yml states the read-only
	     posture plainly.

	     What BADAN does with it: reads lab results only, writes nothing back (`toShare: []` in
	     HealthKitClinicalStore), stores them in badan_sensitive.db under the lab notebook's
	     existing encryption/purge/export gating, and sends nothing off the device — the AI coach
	     reads only non-sensitive derived scores (NonSensitiveDerivation), which structurally
	     cannot resolve this namespace. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit.access</key>
	<array>
		<string>health-records</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.net.binjamshid.badan</string>
	</array>
	<!-- App Attest, for the A9 AI coach proxy at badan.binjamshid.com and nothing else.
	     ADR-0010 deliberately gives that service no accounts, which also leaves it with no
	     per-user identity to rate-limit or authenticate against — the only thing standing
	     between an extracted URL and the founder's OpenRouter bill. App Attest closes that
	     without reintroducing accounts: the device proves the request came from the genuine
	     BADAN binary, and the proof carries no user identity, so it does not widen ADR-0002's
	     posture. It sends nothing about the user and no health data.

	     The entitlement is permission only. It attests nothing until the client generates a
	     DCAppAttestService key and backend/api verifies the attestation; adding the key here
	     does not make the proxy any harder to abuse on its own.

	     $(APP_ATTEST_ENV) is set per configuration in project.yml because the development and
	     production attestation services reject each other's attestation objects — a hardcoded
	     value breaks one half of the matrix as a server-side verification failure, never a
	     build error. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.devicecheck.appattest-environment</key>
	<string>$(APP_ATTEST_ENV)</string>
	<!-- Medication reminders. A "take it now" alert silenced by Sleep Focus is the one class of
	     notification BADAN sends that is worthless late, and Sleep Focus is active during
	     exactly the hours evening/overnight doses fall in. This entitlement permits
	     interruptionLevel = .timeSensitive; it changes nothing until the scheduling code sets
	     the level, and it must stay scoped to meds-due — ring scores and narrative nudges are
	     .active, and using .timeSensitive for them is what makes users revoke the whole
	     category in Settings.

	     Note the product guardrail still applies to the copy: time-sensitive delivery is a
	     scheduling property, not a licence for urgent, diagnostic or prescriptive wording. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.time-sensitive</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Container-wide default file protection. CompleteUntilFirstUserAuthentication, NOT
	     Complete, and the distinction is load-bearing twice over:
	       • HealthKit background delivery wakes BADAN via HKObserverQuery while the device is
	         locked (see the entitlement above). Under NSFileProtectionComplete the anchored
	         re-query cannot open badan.db, and a handler that cannot complete within seconds
	         is exactly the failure ADR-0009 warns is permanent after 3 occurrences.
	       • BadanWidgets reads the shared group container on locked-device timeline reloads
	         and would render empty with no error.
	     This sets the default for files written without an explicit protection attribute; it
	     does not weaken badan_sensitive.db, whose SQLCipher key remains the real gate on the
	     lab notebook and clinical records. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.default-data-protection</key>
	<string>NSFileProtectionCompleteUntilFirstUserAuthentication</string>
</dict>
</plist>
